1
00:00:00,790 --> 00:00:12,762
Namo tassa bhagavato arahato samma sambuddhassa Homage to the Buddha, the blessed, noble and fully self-awakened one.

2
00:00:13,663 --> 00:00:20,111
So I'm afraid this is a wee bit long tonight because once I got going, I couldn't seem to stop.

3
00:00:21,373 --> 00:00:24,115
So it's about faith and it's got a bit of bio in it.

4
00:00:24,356 --> 00:00:25,396
So I hope you don't mind.

5
00:00:26,035 --> 00:00:29,759
So first of all, let's be clear about what this Sada means.

6
00:00:30,300 --> 00:00:33,362
It's not faith and belief.

7
00:00:33,383 --> 00:00:35,384
It's faith as confidence and trust.

8
00:00:35,786 --> 00:00:38,709
So throughout this essay, that's what I mean by faith.

9
00:00:38,789 --> 00:00:42,612
I just like the word faith, confidence and trust.

10
00:00:43,755 --> 00:00:49,860
So I first came across Buddhism while I was teaching in New Guinea at a Roman Catholic school, Papua New Guinea.

11
00:00:50,762 --> 00:00:56,008
Then I read a book by a Franco-German philosopher and philanthropist called Albert Schweitzer.

12
00:00:56,478 --> 00:01:01,924
who argued that Buddhism was life-negating and Christianity was life-affirming.

13
00:01:02,665 --> 00:01:11,777
So it left with a residual dislike of Buddhism, since I was definitely life-affirming, prone to bouts of self-indulgence.

14
00:01:11,796 --> 00:01:22,890
So after my year's teaching, I went down to Sydney, and I can't remember how I came across this book, but it was written by an Irish Jesuit priest called William Johnson, titled Christian Zen.

15
00:01:23,257 --> 00:01:27,765
It was published in 1971, so we're talking about ancient times.

16
00:01:28,606 --> 00:01:36,319
It was 1972 and it struck a chord, so I walked up the road to the shops, being aware of myself all the time.

17
00:01:37,021 --> 00:01:39,004
Well, it felt really odd, so I stopped.

18
00:01:40,846 --> 00:01:49,400
It was only on returning to the UK, around about three years later, when my life fell apart, that this Zen came to mind.

19
00:01:49,685 --> 00:01:52,409
And I wrote to the Buddhist Society in London.

20
00:01:52,469 --> 00:01:58,640
And after a bit of a story, they put me in touch with Vajra Bailey, who held a Zen class.

21
00:01:59,079 --> 00:02:01,024
She's sprinkled in our pond here.

22
00:02:02,525 --> 00:02:10,057
And so she was only living a bus ride away where I was living in Birmingham.

23
00:02:10,078 --> 00:02:15,927
So she led a group in a Zendo, which was a large front bedroom she dedicated to it.

24
00:02:16,194 --> 00:02:18,177
We kneeled on benches facing the wall.

25
00:02:18,518 --> 00:02:24,086
She came over and gave me, shall we say, a gentle punch in the back to straighten the posture.

26
00:02:24,947 --> 00:02:28,331
And then all she said was, watch what comes up.

27
00:02:29,233 --> 00:02:34,621
Well, by the end of that 40 minutes, I was absolutely convinced that this would get me through my crisis.

28
00:02:35,222 --> 00:02:37,044
And I became an aficionado.

29
00:02:37,365 --> 00:02:40,449
And my life just took on meaning.

30
00:02:40,628 --> 00:02:42,692
I felt elated, purposeful.

31
00:02:43,145 --> 00:02:48,835
So now most of you, I'm sure, have been through that stage and it's called serene or clearing faith.

32
00:02:49,256 --> 00:02:49,757
It's the first.

33
00:02:50,076 --> 00:02:52,161
There's four levels of faith, you see.

34
00:02:52,641 --> 00:02:56,549
So the first one is known as this serene or clearing faith.

35
00:02:56,709 --> 00:03:01,598
Although I do like Roshi Suzuki's word for it, beginner's mind.

36
00:03:02,699 --> 00:03:04,342
Some of you will know that classic book.

37
00:03:04,980 --> 00:03:13,731
So it's serene because you really feel happy you found a path and clearing because it gives you direction where before you were sort of searching here and there.

38
00:03:14,612 --> 00:03:21,281
Now, the greater the effect, the greater the danger that after the initial phase dies down, the meditator stops practicing.

39
00:03:22,081 --> 00:03:32,354
For although the effects can change a person's life quite radically, as the sense of progress halters and the practice becomes boring, that's when they leave.

40
00:03:33,365 --> 00:03:41,997
But if after this phase there's a continuing interest in the Dharma and a deepening of practice, especially through retreats, then the faith becomes firmer.

41
00:03:42,519 --> 00:03:46,264
So much here now depends upon a guiding teacher, a supportive group.

42
00:03:46,885 --> 00:03:50,091
So this is all new stuff and we all need support and direction.

43
00:03:51,217 --> 00:03:58,706
So I was really quite fortunate to find this teacher with a dedicated zendo who was knowledgeable.

44
00:03:58,727 --> 00:03:59,287
She was good.

45
00:03:59,306 --> 00:04:00,288
She was a good teacher.

46
00:04:00,829 --> 00:04:04,272
And more, there was a monastery, Throstle Hole Abbey.

47
00:04:04,933 --> 00:04:14,745
So at that time, my interests were fueled by reading people like Alan Watts and Christmas Humphreys, Rahula Walpola, which I don't think many people know now.

48
00:04:15,247 --> 00:04:24,788
Though the amounts of books on Buddhism were really small compared to today with the deluge of books, articles and websites.

49
00:04:24,848 --> 00:04:31,401
So as I began to understand the teaching of the Four Noble Truths and karma, my faith grew to a convinced confidence.

50
00:04:31,634 --> 00:04:33,437
and especially through the practice of Zen.

51
00:04:34,218 --> 00:04:45,615
So much so that after a year I took Jukai, that means taking refuges and precepts, and I still have my wagesa, it's like a collar, which symbolizes the monastic rogues.

52
00:04:46,456 --> 00:04:49,781
So this level is called convinced or anchored faith.

53
00:04:51,423 --> 00:04:53,747
Now one can practice from two bases.

54
00:04:53,867 --> 00:04:58,113
There are those who are liberated by faith and those liberated by wisdom.

55
00:04:58,432 --> 00:05:10,247
Now, all that means is that everybody has a natural inclination, a bit more towards faith and devotion, or a bit more towards the intellect.

56
00:05:10,307 --> 00:05:12,951
But, I mean, we've both got some of each.

57
00:05:14,134 --> 00:05:25,889
So some get connected through the chanting, connection with a teacher, a heart connection, through reading, contemplating the life of the Buddha, famous teachers like Ajahn Mahabhua, Mechi Kiao,

58
00:05:26,562 --> 00:05:35,172
So it inspires them to practice and experience direct experiential understanding without too much, even very little, intellectual knowledge.

59
00:05:35,992 --> 00:05:39,495
Now, the classic example of this is Chulapantaka.

60
00:05:39,516 --> 00:05:44,601
So he'd followed his very clever brother into the order.

61
00:05:45,302 --> 00:05:53,531
However, he was so dull-minded that he said of himself, when he learnt a new verse from the Buddha, it knocked the one he'd just learnt out of his head.

62
00:05:54,288 --> 00:05:58,331
So his brother said, well, you know, he may as well go back to lay life.

63
00:05:58,692 --> 00:06:00,293
When the Buddha heard this, he went to see him.

64
00:06:01,095 --> 00:06:10,684
Comforting him, he gave him a very simple exercise to take a clean cloth and then just keep rubbing it all the while saying, removing the dust, removing the dust.

65
00:06:11,524 --> 00:06:17,591
So as he saw the dust becoming more and more soiled, he realized that his heart was similarly soiled by defilements.

66
00:06:18,331 --> 00:06:20,093
So he began to purify the heart.

67
00:06:20,444 --> 00:06:30,041
and regained its original purity, realizing the cause of the defilements was unwholesome desire driven by a false identity of this self.

68
00:06:30,081 --> 00:06:31,605
He awakened, fully liberated.

69
00:06:32,105 --> 00:06:33,829
So, there's hope for us all, eh?

70
00:06:35,853 --> 00:06:41,202
Now, there are those who are drawn by understanding the Dharma, so they're driven by intellectual curiosity.

71
00:06:41,452 --> 00:06:51,824
They still have to purify the heart of defilements, but insights come from careful investigation in their practice based upon that intellectual understanding.

72
00:06:52,564 --> 00:06:55,348
So this is very much like a scientist might work.

73
00:06:55,367 --> 00:07:00,293
You see, the classical example is Sariputta, whom the Buddha named General of the Dharma.

74
00:07:00,494 --> 00:07:08,423
He was able to discern all the moments, all the little stages in the practice of absorptions, the jhana, and the practice of vipassana.

75
00:07:08,959 --> 00:07:12,125
It took him two whole weeks to achieve liberation.

76
00:07:13,045 --> 00:07:21,677
And this is contrasted with Moggallana, the other chief disciple, who was devotional, driven by absorptions, vajana, heartfelt states.

77
00:07:22,338 --> 00:07:23,281
He only took a week.

78
00:07:24,762 --> 00:07:38,021
And he gained the six supernatural powers, clairvoyance, clairaudience, mind reading, recollection of past lives, various other powers, and most important, the destruction of the defilements.

79
00:07:39,266 --> 00:07:40,608
So now both have their danger.

80
00:07:40,867 --> 00:07:45,112
The faith liberated can become drowned in devotion and just be happy to stay there.

81
00:07:46,052 --> 00:07:54,399
And the wisdom liberated can fall into the delusion that they understand the Dharma so well, might even have written a book, you know, that they must be liberated.

82
00:07:55,161 --> 00:07:57,702
So a classic example is the monk Potila.

83
00:07:58,223 --> 00:07:59,285
He's an excellent teacher.

84
00:07:59,545 --> 00:08:02,166
Many of his students became liberated, but not him.

85
00:08:03,267 --> 00:08:08,952
And the Buddha actually called him Manga Potila, which means empty Potila or useless Potila.

86
00:08:09,406 --> 00:08:18,259
So swallowing his pride, he went to one of his students for guidance, but the student realised he was too intellectually conceited and he said he couldn't help him.

87
00:08:18,860 --> 00:08:30,300
This happened with all his other liberated students until at last one of them directed him to a young boy, a Samanera, seven years old, eight years old, who was liberated.

88
00:08:30,937 --> 00:08:40,650
So again, swallowing his pride more deeply, he listened to the boy who asked him, how would he get a snake out of an anthill which has six openings?

89
00:08:41,331 --> 00:08:44,475
So snakes often live in abandoned anthills.

90
00:08:44,495 --> 00:08:48,400
Portilla said, well, all you have to do is close up the five holes.

91
00:08:49,241 --> 00:08:51,164
And the boy told him, well, that's what you've got to do.

92
00:08:51,184 --> 00:08:54,529
You've got to close up the five outer senses and watch the mind.

93
00:08:55,009 --> 00:08:59,375
Well, in no length of time, Portilla became one of the Garahats.

94
00:09:01,177 --> 00:09:03,621
So this is something we can reflect upon.

95
00:09:03,822 --> 00:09:04,803
What is my leaning?

96
00:09:04,844 --> 00:09:06,145
Am I a devotional type?

97
00:09:06,206 --> 00:09:07,467
More the intellectual type?

98
00:09:07,528 --> 00:09:11,533
Both demand the practice of moment-to-moment mindfulness and vipassana.

99
00:09:13,096 --> 00:09:19,346
But the relief here is that the devotional type need not be worried if they don't understand the finer points of the Dharma.

100
00:09:20,147 --> 00:09:24,874
And the intellectual type need not be concerned that they don't have much devotional feelings.

101
00:09:26,794 --> 00:09:33,668
So at this stage, the possibility of falling away from the practice is still there.

102
00:09:33,707 --> 00:09:44,769
If in the first phase it was just lack of progress after the initial inspiration, here is the great enemy of faith, doubt, and a special type of doubt, sceptical doubt.

103
00:09:45,457 --> 00:09:52,785
So wholesome doubt that leads to careful investigation, continued practice is exactly what the Buddha expects us to do.

104
00:09:53,606 --> 00:10:00,714
And he says all that in the Kalama Sutta, which, if you remember, we did last week, last month.

105
00:10:01,654 --> 00:10:10,423
And he tells us not to believe anything whatsoever until we ourselves know by our own direct experience whether it's true or not.

106
00:10:10,445 --> 00:10:14,688
And indeed, it's that very exploration, remember, that leads to liberation.

107
00:10:15,277 --> 00:10:17,739
cuts through the delusion.

108
00:10:19,260 --> 00:10:25,888
So now doubts can arise about the Buddha, the Dharma, the Sangha, one's teacher or lineage, and of course oneself.

109
00:10:26,607 --> 00:10:29,390
So the Buddha said he was fully liberated from suffering.

110
00:10:29,451 --> 00:10:30,993
He said the end game was Nibbana.

111
00:10:31,013 --> 00:10:34,076
But how do we know that there isn't something beyond?

112
00:10:34,735 --> 00:10:36,418
And it doesn't sound so good to me.

113
00:10:36,717 --> 00:10:40,542
Either it's some sort of unconsciousness or an awareness of aware of nothing.

114
00:10:41,230 --> 00:10:42,172
And what about God?

115
00:10:42,351 --> 00:10:46,238
What sort of universal intelligence and heart that we're all part of?

116
00:10:46,538 --> 00:10:52,849
I mean, what sort of doubts have you come up with in yourself about the Buddha Dhamma?

117
00:10:52,869 --> 00:10:55,874
So the Buddha never presented his teachings as thou shalt believe.

118
00:10:55,894 --> 00:10:57,956
It was always explained, given encouragement.

119
00:10:58,357 --> 00:11:03,605
So whenever people approached him with opposite views, he would listen, point out the weakness of their argument.

120
00:11:03,946 --> 00:11:11,336
Once the person sort of accepted that criticism and were ready to listen, then, of course, he would explain his own understanding.

121
00:11:12,078 --> 00:11:12,879
So it is with us.

122
00:11:12,960 --> 00:11:19,950
When we come across teachings we don't agree with, you see, then, for instance, the stumbling block, the usual stumbling block of rebirth.

123
00:11:20,630 --> 00:11:25,057
So we don't use the word reincarnation so as not to be confused with Hindu teachings.

124
00:11:25,493 --> 00:11:34,847
Actually, the word reincarnation literally means reborn in the flesh, whereas rebirth simply means life after death.

125
00:11:34,868 --> 00:11:40,857
Now, we may be of the convinced opinion that this is not possible since we're only the body.

126
00:11:42,080 --> 00:11:49,812
So I was once on a train going out of London and a football crowd came in.

127
00:11:50,671 --> 00:11:53,595
And they sat right opposite me, these two men.

128
00:11:54,115 --> 00:11:56,317
And one of them said to me, are you a Buddhist monk?

129
00:11:56,357 --> 00:11:57,298
And I said, yes, I am.

130
00:11:57,379 --> 00:11:59,182
He said, well, I don't believe in reincarnation.

131
00:12:01,404 --> 00:12:02,586
It was as direct as that.

132
00:12:03,807 --> 00:12:08,594
And I said to him, do you actually know what happened?

133
00:12:09,815 --> 00:12:12,678
You know, whether there is life after death or not.

134
00:12:13,418 --> 00:12:16,682
So, you know, he said he hadn't had any experience like that.

135
00:12:17,322 --> 00:12:20,365
And he tacitly agreed with me that he didn't know.

136
00:12:20,947 --> 00:12:23,230
So I said to him, well, why come to a conclusion?

137
00:12:23,669 --> 00:12:27,094
And his friend turned to him and repeated, yeah, why come to a conclusion?

138
00:12:27,955 --> 00:12:43,273
So when we come across these sorts of teachings that we don't, that doesn't agree with our particular understanding, instead of denying them or feeling we have to give them the benefits of the doubt, just leave them on the back burner, you know, wait for more experiential information.

139
00:12:43,625 --> 00:13:08,602
through our practice you know it might not come till we're dead you know so there's also faith in the sangha now here the word does not refer to the ordained monks and nuns but to the ariya sangha the noble sangha those who've attained one of the powers and fruits and that of course includes lay people here ever since the time of the Buddha there have always been those who've achieved these attainments

140
00:13:08,852 --> 00:13:20,109
According to the tradition, once the dispensation fails to produce such exemplars, the teachings would become intellectual and devotional and eventually just empty ritual.

141
00:13:21,210 --> 00:13:27,558
So they are the inspiration that infuses the whole dispensation, such as the Mahaji Sayadaw in our tradition.

142
00:13:28,460 --> 00:13:34,168
And they're the ones who keep reaching the heights and become assurances for us that we can also achieve that.

143
00:13:35,818 --> 00:13:39,784
When it comes to teachers, there have been some unfortunate, shameful cases.

144
00:13:39,865 --> 00:13:48,177
I think I'm right in saying that all around sexual behavior, mostly male teachers abusing, taking advantage of women devotees.

145
00:13:48,979 --> 00:13:52,063
So in general, one tends to idolize the teacher.

146
00:13:53,065 --> 00:13:59,514
I mean, they are someone special in our lives and we expect them to behave to standards that they ask of us.

147
00:14:00,456 --> 00:14:08,509
Now, in such a case, the student may be overcome by discouragement and doubt in the teacher when on reflection.

148
00:14:09,311 --> 00:14:23,394
If they have a doubt in the teacher, they might overcome that doubt when they remember the benefits of the teaching, and they may continue to stay with the teacher, acknowledging them as a good spiritual guide.

149
00:14:23,813 --> 00:14:24,355
However,

150
00:14:24,908 --> 00:14:30,977
When there's a serious breach of conduct, that can make the student even doubt the Dhamma.

151
00:14:31,859 --> 00:14:38,710
Consider how you would feel if information from various sources proves that the Buddha was having affairs with many nuns and monks.

152
00:14:39,892 --> 00:14:45,039
So there was a case, actually, where it was discovered an abbot had a long affair with one of his students.

153
00:14:45,480 --> 00:14:46,863
They just broke the community.

154
00:14:48,807 --> 00:14:51,591
But in general, most overcome their disenchantment

155
00:14:51,975 --> 00:14:56,484
they have over their teachers, just recognising that they expected too much.

156
00:14:56,884 --> 00:14:59,590
That's what happens when you idolise somebody.

157
00:15:00,150 --> 00:15:00,751
I mean, I did.

158
00:15:03,496 --> 00:15:10,068
Sceptical doubt becomes difficult to overcome, especially when it is doubt in ourselves.

159
00:15:10,504 --> 00:15:22,759
So whatever the cause is within our upbringing and life so far, what we may be left with is a lack of confidence in our own reasoning, decision-making, even how we feel about ourselves, our self-worth.

160
00:15:23,260 --> 00:15:25,322
And this tends to be projected onto others.

161
00:15:25,764 --> 00:15:31,711
So we feel lessened in people's eyes and so our self-esteem lessens.

162
00:15:31,730 --> 00:15:33,373
So it can affect our lives deeply.

163
00:15:33,413 --> 00:15:39,921
We go for a job, get it, and then doubt we can do it, even though actually getting the job probably means we can do it very well.

164
00:15:40,576 --> 00:15:45,385
We have a growing relationship with someone, but then can't decide to form a close relationship.

165
00:15:45,826 --> 00:15:50,476
The relationship fails and further reinforces our self-doubt.

166
00:15:51,337 --> 00:15:52,620
It can be a fear of failure.

167
00:15:52,860 --> 00:15:59,394
So the person sets impossible perfections and just reinforcing that self-belief.

168
00:16:00,217 --> 00:16:03,263
And it can lead to chronic anxiety about everything we do.

169
00:16:03,302 --> 00:16:05,066
Is this what I ought to be doing?

170
00:16:05,125 --> 00:16:05,687
Is it right?

171
00:16:05,807 --> 00:16:06,288
Is it wrong?

172
00:16:06,327 --> 00:16:06,788
Is it up?

173
00:16:06,808 --> 00:16:07,470
Is it down?

174
00:16:08,091 --> 00:16:15,062
It can actually become a form of mental illness called imposterism, where the person feels themselves to be false or pretense.

175
00:16:15,964 --> 00:16:22,615
So I think we all suffer from some level of doubt, but hopefully it doesn't prevent us from saying and doing what we ultimately decide.

176
00:16:23,741 --> 00:16:27,687
So in my own life, you see, it took me two years to decide to join the Sangha.

177
00:16:27,706 --> 00:16:29,590
And then I determined only three years.

178
00:16:29,870 --> 00:16:30,671
I don't know what happened.

179
00:16:31,592 --> 00:16:33,235
It was such a radical thing to do.

180
00:16:33,254 --> 00:16:37,340
I was building up a career in teaching, owned a house and had a growing relationship.

181
00:16:38,020 --> 00:16:40,124
So the desire kept scratching my brain.

182
00:16:40,224 --> 00:16:48,335
Finally, I went to see my teacher in the Birmingham Buddhist Bihara, Sayadaw Urewitadamba, who was a Burmese monk.

183
00:16:49,296 --> 00:16:50,457
And he said,

184
00:16:50,759 --> 00:16:51,500
I was too old.

185
00:16:52,022 --> 00:16:52,562
I was 37.

186
00:16:53,244 --> 00:16:55,008
Well, frankly, it came as a great relief.

187
00:16:55,428 --> 00:16:57,432
Right, I thought, now I can get on with my life.

188
00:16:58,134 --> 00:17:00,860
But the fact was that the urge just wouldn't leave me.

189
00:17:01,159 --> 00:17:10,219
And I kept thinking how wonderful it would be to spend just three years in an establishment that supported you to just meditate, meditate, meditate.

190
00:17:10,823 --> 00:17:12,605
So after six months, I went to see him again.

191
00:17:13,125 --> 00:17:21,096
This time he said, well, now that I was a teacher, I was doing sort of beginners classes and stuff, that I was needed at the centre.

192
00:17:21,316 --> 00:17:23,960
So again, I went away, but the relief wasn't so great.

193
00:17:24,420 --> 00:17:27,703
And indeed, I think there was a touch of disappointment at that time.

194
00:17:28,625 --> 00:17:34,972
Time passed, again, the urge, you know, not a simple desire, just really cloying, wouldn't leave me.

195
00:17:35,173 --> 00:17:36,796
So I trusted in the practice.

196
00:17:37,375 --> 00:17:39,378
I had to stop thinking about it.

197
00:17:40,034 --> 00:17:47,744
I hired a bungalow in the heart of Wales that a friend owned and went there for two weeks.

198
00:17:47,766 --> 00:17:52,952
And I just, you know, I just stopped all that pros and cons business and just sat.

199
00:17:53,594 --> 00:17:57,599
By the end of two weeks, my heart spoke with absolute clarity.

200
00:17:57,921 --> 00:18:00,344
When I went to see my teacher again, he just said, OK.

201
00:18:02,887 --> 00:18:05,551
But at least it was an exercise in being certain.

202
00:18:06,747 --> 00:18:10,391
So skeptical doubt, of course, is the last of the five hindrances.

203
00:18:10,531 --> 00:18:18,221
So when we find ourselves in this sort of doubt, it's always wise not to get caught up in those mental proliferations, the ruminating.

204
00:18:19,162 --> 00:18:26,951
Rather, as always, just see it as an occasion to heal oneself of that gnawing doubt by feeling it in the body, usually around the chest, yeah?

205
00:18:27,172 --> 00:18:28,913
And just sink into the agitation.

206
00:18:29,555 --> 00:18:35,201
Investigate the various tones of the sensations, insubstantial, impermanent, and so on.

207
00:18:35,688 --> 00:18:38,652
There's no need to try and fix the problem.

208
00:18:38,992 --> 00:18:44,539
See, by allowing the emotion to manifest, that turbulence is discharged.

209
00:18:45,181 --> 00:18:46,643
And remember, no energy is lost.

210
00:18:46,702 --> 00:18:50,028
It's always transformed to the opposite, to the good one.

211
00:18:51,068 --> 00:18:57,939
And when it lessens and passes, just acknowledge the relief and perhaps the equanimity and the clarity that arises.

212
00:18:58,839 --> 00:19:04,867
Even if only we've learned how skeptical doubt works and operates and fools us.

213
00:19:06,568 --> 00:19:19,925
Of course, again, positive doubt, curiosity, the wonder of an artist, the philosopher, the scientist, just galvanizes our investigation into the Dharma, both the understanding and the insight practice.

214
00:19:20,967 --> 00:19:25,853
Now, the third level is called the accumulated or aspirational faith.

215
00:19:25,873 --> 00:19:30,598
This only applies to people who've taken the vow to become fully self-awakened Buddhas.

216
00:19:31,098 --> 00:19:33,942
So if there's anybody amongst you who's done that,

217
00:19:36,032 --> 00:19:45,541
That is, you have to be reborn at a time when the dispensation has disappeared and the person has all the qualities needed to become self-awakened.

218
00:19:45,561 --> 00:19:51,307
So in Theravada, the Bodhisattva vow is not the same as the Mahayana Bodhisattva vow.

219
00:19:51,708 --> 00:19:57,532
So they've transformed the vow into refusing to be fully liberated till all beings are liberated, right?

220
00:19:57,553 --> 00:20:00,096
It's a sort of great compassion thing.

221
00:20:00,115 --> 00:20:03,558
I'm not sure how that pans out since all beings are meant to be innumerable.

222
00:20:03,775 --> 00:20:06,357
But I think at best to see it as an aspiration.

223
00:20:07,480 --> 00:20:11,846
So there was a famous monk in Sri Lanka, Venerable Balangoda Anandamatreya.

224
00:20:12,946 --> 00:20:21,157
He was on retreat with the Mahasi Sayadaw when the monastery I stayed at, Kandaboda, was opened in the mid-50s, 1950s.

225
00:20:22,720 --> 00:20:28,347
And during his rise through the Vipassananyanas, you see, you arrive at a place of equanimity.

226
00:20:28,884 --> 00:20:35,152
which is just before you have the insight, you see, which brings you to a level of attainment.

227
00:20:35,692 --> 00:20:41,479
And he remembered that he'd taken this Bodhisattva vow at the time of the Buddha.

228
00:20:41,499 --> 00:20:46,084
He said he remembered the faces of Sariputta and Moggallana, but not the Buddha.

229
00:20:46,305 --> 00:20:47,145
It's a shame, you know.

230
00:20:48,106 --> 00:20:50,650
According to the tradition, he can't go any further.

231
00:20:50,789 --> 00:20:51,730
He has to stop there.

232
00:20:52,352 --> 00:20:57,136
He will now take many rebirths during which he will hone all the ten perfections

233
00:20:57,538 --> 00:21:03,268
to the point that he'll be ready to be reborn as a human being, as a man, unfortunately.

234
00:21:06,353 --> 00:21:10,499
Because in the tradition, remember, time is circular.

235
00:21:11,019 --> 00:21:13,825
So you always have the same situations repeating.

236
00:21:14,144 --> 00:21:21,656
Those of you who know the 28 past Buddhas, they've all got Pali names and they've all got two famous disciples.

237
00:21:21,973 --> 00:21:36,634
So anyway, when he's finally reborn, he'll be able to go through the piles of fruits by himself, like Siddhartha Gautama did, and then establish a dispensation.

238
00:21:37,454 --> 00:21:42,701
So he was a professor, vice-chancellor of the monastic Vidyadaya University.

239
00:21:44,304 --> 00:21:50,192
So he was driven by that sort of vow somewhere in his consciousness.

240
00:21:51,151 --> 00:21:56,855
But again, it's not absolutely secure because he's not attained one of the paths and fruits.

241
00:21:58,176 --> 00:22:00,358
Skeptical doubt can still come up, yeah.

242
00:22:00,378 --> 00:22:08,465
And he said of himself that he was honing the quality of understanding in this life.

243
00:22:08,486 --> 00:22:20,195
Now, for those of you who are unacquainted with these paths and fruits, in short, they're all attained by insights into one of the three characteristics of existence, impermanence, unsatisfactoriness, not self.

244
00:22:20,699 --> 00:22:24,884
So the first is called stream entrant, where all doubt in the Buddha Dhamma disappears.

245
00:22:25,425 --> 00:22:34,900
The second insight into one of those three characteristics goes deeper, lessens the power of unwholesome desires over us.

246
00:22:35,059 --> 00:22:40,307
Although the stream entrant must go through seven rebirths, the once returner, of course, is almost there.

247
00:22:41,210 --> 00:22:48,580
The third one, the non-returner, is no longer attracted to sensual desires and therefore doesn't become a human being anymore.

248
00:22:48,601 --> 00:22:50,182
He remains in the higher realms.

249
00:22:50,398 --> 00:22:53,603
And the Arhat, of course, is liberated from all becoming.

250
00:22:54,804 --> 00:23:05,336
So we've come to the final, the fourth level, the realized or unshakable faith, which is when we become one of these, when we become a stream entrant, right?

251
00:23:05,356 --> 00:23:06,558
That's the beginning.

252
00:23:06,598 --> 00:23:08,280
That's the entrance into the path.

253
00:23:09,321 --> 00:23:14,607
Such is the direct experiential knowledge of one of those three characteristics of existence.

254
00:23:14,669 --> 00:23:17,471
It dispels all doubt in the Buddha Dhamma.

255
00:23:17,491 --> 00:23:20,035
Some are happy and satisfied to have attained this path.

256
00:23:20,403 --> 00:23:28,290
After all, it's a great achievement to have risen out of the delusion that's caused persistent rebirth and sometimes into the animal and hell realm.

257
00:23:28,511 --> 00:23:32,556
That's not possible anymore for somebody who's achieved this path and fruit.

258
00:23:33,936 --> 00:23:38,261
And for some, it becomes an inspiration, of course, and a goal to continue.

259
00:23:39,542 --> 00:23:44,288
So as faith in the Buddha Dharma grows, so there comes a time when a person wants to commit themselves.

260
00:23:44,709 --> 00:23:47,912
Now, traditionally, this is done by taking refuges and precepts.

261
00:23:48,500 --> 00:23:51,683
So the refuges, of course, are the Buddha Dhamma Sangha.

262
00:23:51,703 --> 00:23:58,711
When the first mendicants wanted to follow the Buddha, he simply said, Ehi Pasiko, come and try.

263
00:23:59,152 --> 00:24:00,513
It's called the First Ordination.

264
00:24:01,795 --> 00:24:08,323
And after that, when the Sangha started to produce Arahats, it became what we now call the Three Refuges.

265
00:24:08,843 --> 00:24:11,506
And it eventually became the Lay Ordination.

266
00:24:12,426 --> 00:24:17,393
As people joined the Sangha who were not so pure-hearted, who were not so, shall we say, advanced,

267
00:24:17,811 --> 00:24:20,655
the Buddha started to make rules as safeguards.

268
00:24:20,675 --> 00:24:23,278
And eventually the rules of the Vinaya grew to 227.

269
00:24:23,420 --> 00:24:29,367
So the monastic ordination really becomes quite an intricate ceremony.

270
00:24:31,029 --> 00:24:34,253
So everyone will have their own reasons for taking refuge as a precept.

271
00:24:34,334 --> 00:24:36,376
In my own case, it came as a great relief.

272
00:24:36,998 --> 00:24:48,929
Having left the Catholic Church, which had given me a sort of moral and devotional basis, I was drawn to the atheistic existentialist, found at least a reason to live, may as well make a go of life.

273
00:24:50,230 --> 00:24:56,375
But with my life crisis, it took away, I looked for a way to heal the heart suffused with turbulence.

274
00:24:56,436 --> 00:25:01,902
And at that time, therapy as we know it today was hardly known, or at least I didn't know about it.

275
00:25:02,321 --> 00:25:05,384
And I probably would have thought of it more in terms of psychiatry.

276
00:25:06,074 --> 00:25:07,536
I think this was lucky.

277
00:25:08,217 --> 00:25:12,262
For these days, my first port of call would have been a therapy or counselling.

278
00:25:13,163 --> 00:25:18,951
This may very well have helped me out of the hole I was in, but it would not have given me the spiritual depth of Buddhism.

279
00:25:19,593 --> 00:25:24,519
I may never have found a spiritual path as such, but remained happy with just being okay.

280
00:25:25,560 --> 00:25:33,211
Buddha Dharma offered me something more, Nibbāna, that was transcendence, and taking refuges and precepts gave me a focus.

281
00:25:33,647 --> 00:25:37,512
From then on, everything I did had to be in line with the Buddha Dharma.

282
00:25:37,633 --> 00:25:43,821
So there's no problem with reading other spiritual texts, with yoga, qigong, all sorts of therapies.

283
00:25:44,502 --> 00:25:50,290
Anything is viable so long as I feel it is helping me along the path.

284
00:25:50,310 --> 00:25:59,182
So, faith is foundational, it's linked, it's always brought up together with insight, wisdom.

285
00:26:00,277 --> 00:26:13,631
So faith gives us that courage and commitment to practice in the path of purification, the commentary by Buddhaghosa, it is said it is like a water purifying gem dispelling fogginess of delusion and doubt.

286
00:26:14,711 --> 00:26:26,044
The practice brings insight which confirms the faith and there comes a point where faith becomes steadfast on the ground of insight into impermanence, reactivity and not-self.

287
00:26:26,243 --> 00:26:26,284
So

288
00:26:26,702 --> 00:26:29,247
Where are you when it comes to faith in the Buddha Dhamma?

289
00:26:29,267 --> 00:26:30,650
See, is it really established?

290
00:26:30,710 --> 00:26:32,291
Have you taken refuge in the priesthood?

291
00:26:32,512 --> 00:26:34,154
If not, why not?

292
00:26:36,239 --> 00:26:49,520
I can only hope my words have been of some assistance and they have not brought confusion and that by your devotion to the practice you will be liberated from all suffering sooner rather than later.

